<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sri Aurobindo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1872">1872</a>–<a href="/years/1950">1950</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Aurobindo Ghosh</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sri Aurobindo, born Aurobindo Ghosh in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a> in 1872, was an Indian philosopher, yogi and nationalist. After study at King's College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, he served the princely state of Baroda from 1893 and moved into nationalist politics in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, advocating complete autonomy from British rule. Arrested in May 1908 in the Alipore Bomb Case, he was acquitted on 6 May 1909 after a year of isolated incarceration, and in April 1910 withdrew to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pondicherry">Pondicherry</a>, then a French colony. There he developed the spiritual practice he called Integral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoga">Yoga</a>, wrote The Life Divine, The Synthesis of Yoga and the epic poem Savitri, and in 1926 founded the Sri Aurobindo Ashram with Mirra Alfassa.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sri-aurobindo.jpg" alt="Sri Aurobindo" width="330" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rudolf 1922 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sri_aurobindo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the 19th and early 20th centuries, Dayananda Saraswati, who in Robson's account held that the Vedas contained no errors, and <b>Sri Aurobindo</b>, who thought Sayana too ritualistic, read the hymns as monotheistic or mystical.</blockquote><a href="/work/rigveda">Rigveda · Reception</a></li><li><blockquote>The poem's first meaning, Bengal or all India, is debated: <b>Sri Aurobindo</b> called it the &quot;National Anthem of Bengal&quot;, while most parties and historians stress its pan-Indian role.</blockquote><a href="/songs/vande-mataram">Vande Mataram</a></li></ul></section>
